{title: Lead, Kindly Light}
{artist: John Henry Newman / John B. Dykes}
{genre: Hymn}
{style: Traditional}
{flow: Meditative}
{key: G}
{key_original: Ab}
{tempo: 95}
{time: 3/4}
{c: Chords checked against the four-part harmony of the Cyber Hymnal score (Book of Worship for United States Forces (Washington, DC: U.S. Government Printing Office, 1974), # 119)}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[G]Lead, kindly Light, a[Am]mid th’en[C]circling gloom,
[G]Lead Thou me [D7]on!
[D7]The night is [G]dark, and [Am]I am [C]far from home;__[G]__
[G]Lead [D7]Thou me on!__[G]__
[D]Keep Thou my [A7]feet; [D]I do not [A7]ask to__[D7]__ see
[G]The distant [C]scene; one step e[G]nough__[D7]__ for me.__[G]__
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[G]I was not ever [Am]thus, nor [C]prayed that Thou
[G]Shouldst lead me [D7]on;
[D7]I loved to [G]choose and [Am]see my [C]path; but now__[G]__
[G]Lead [D7]Thou me on!__[G]__
[D]I loved the [A7]gar[D]ish day, and, [A7]spite of__[D7]__ fears,
[G]Pride ruled my [C]will. Remember [G]not__[D7]__ past years!__[G]__
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[G]So long Thy power hath [Am]blest me, [C]sure it still
[G]Will lead me [D7]on.
[D7]O’er moor and [G]fen, o’er [Am]crag and [C]torrent,
[C]Till__[G]__ the [D7]night is gone,__[G]__
[D]And with the [A7]morn [D]those angel [A7]faces__[D7]__ smile, [G]which I
[G]Have [C]loved long since, and [G]lost__[D7]__ awhile!__[G]__
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[G]Meantime, along the [Am]narrow [C]rugged path,
[G]Thyself hast [D7]trod,
[D7]Lead, Savior, [G]lead me [Am]home in [C]childlike faith,__[G]__
[G]Home [D7]to my God.__[G]__
[D]To rest for[A7]ev[D]er after [A7]earthly__[D7]__ strife
[G]In the calm [C]light of ever[G]last__[D7]__ing life.__[G]__
{end_of_verse}
